[05:32:27] │  summary="The normative rule norm:mmode_req mandates that all RISC-V hardware implementations must provide M-mode. Violating this requirement by omitting or disabling the highest privilege level constitutes a privilege_escape vulnerability: every security mechanism rooted in M-mode—including PMP-based memory isolation, trap delegation, interrupt control, and the SBI firmware interface—is eliminated. The absence of M-mode removes the architectural root of trust for the entire privilege hierarchy, leaving lower privilege levels with no hardware-enforced isolation boundary and no recovery path. This pattern is detectable in CPU RTL (checking for absence of M-mode logic), firmware (checking whether M-mode is assumed but not provided), and OS kernels (checking SBI call dependencies on M-mode services)." entities=['CSR misa (Machine ISA Register) — controls available ISA features; inaccessible without M-mode', 'CSR mstatus (Machine Status Register) — controls global interrupt enables and privilege-level context; inaccessible without M-mode', 'CSR medeleg (Machine Exception Delegation Register) — controls exception delegation from M-mode to S-mode; no delegation logic functions without M-mode', 'CSR mideleg (Machine Interrupt Delegation Register) — controls interrupt delegation from M-mode to S-mode; all interrupts must be handled in M-mode with no delegation path', 'Physical Memory Protection (PMP) CSRs (pmpcfg0-pmpcfg15, pmpaddr0-pmpaddr63) — enforces physical memory access control; absent without M-mode, allowing unrestricted physical memory access from all privilege levels', 'M-mode trap handler vector (mtvec) — defines the entry point for all machine-level exceptions and interrupts; no trap handling without M-mode', 'M-mode interrupt-related CSRs (mie, mip) — machine-level interrupt enable and pending registers; all interrupt management lost', 'M-mode scratch/exception-program-counter CSRs (mscratch, mepc) — context-switch and return-address registers for M-mode traps; no machine-level context management', 'M-mode cause/value CSRs (mcause, mtval) — trap cause classification and faulting address capture; lost diagnostic and recovery capability', 'Firmware layer (OpenSBI, U-Boot SPL, ARM Trusted Firmware equivalents, EDK2) — assumes M-mode exists for secure boot, runtime services, and power management; cannot initialize or operate correctly', 'OS kernel (Linux RISC-V, FreeBSD RISC-V) — assumes M-mode provides supervisor binary interface (SBI) services, timer interrupts, and inter-processor interrupts; kernel boot and runtime fail or operate with degraded security', 'Bare-metal applications — assumed to run in M-mode for direct hardware control; unexpected privilege confinement breaks device access and performance counter usage', 'Hardware debugging and trace infrastructure — typically relies on M-mode for debug-mode entry and external debugger access; debug capabilities lost']
[05:32:27] ╰─ Phase 1 done.
